  "query_preview": "..\nThursday night through Saturday: Upper-level ridging strengthens \nover the Inland Northwest Thursday night into Friday. This will keep \nweather dry with temperatures warming on Friday into the mid 60s to \nmid 70s. \n\nChanges in the pattern arrive Saturday as a quick moving cold front \nmoves through the Pacific Northwest. Moisture will be rather limited \nwith this front, with nearly all of the precipitation falling west \nof and at the Cascade crest. There is a small chance for very light \nrain or sprinkles across northeast Washington with the front, but \namounts will be negligible. \n\nThe primary hazard with this front will be the winds as the PDX-GPI \npressure gradient increases to +9-13mb and 850mb winds increase to \naround 25 to 35 mph. This will support sustained winds 15 to 25 mph \nwith gusts 25 to 40 mph across the lee of the Cascades, the Columbia \nBasin, the Spokane/CdA area and the Washington and Idaho Palouse \nlate Saturday morning through the afternoon. The National Blend of \nModels (NBM) probabilities supports this south a widespread 50% or \ngreater for wind gusts greater than 30 MPH across the aforementioned \nareas, with an 80% chance or greater across the Waterville Plateau \nand the upper Columbia Basin. A lack of precipitation in recent \nweeks across the Columbia Basin, patchy blowing dust will be a \nconcern along with choppy lakes, crosswinds, and airborne Halloween \ndecorations that are not tied down. \n\nSunday through Wednesday: Quiet weather returns for Sunday with \ntemperatures cooling by 3-7 degrees and much lighter winds. \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance continue to depict an upper-\nlevel trough digging along the west coast early next week, with the \nbase digging into a closed low over California. This evolution will \nleave the Pacific Northwest under subtle ridging before the\nupper-level low gets picked back up into the main flow later in\nthe week. Aside from low precipitation chances (10 to 15%) \nacross the Cascade crest Monday night, shifting to the Blue \nMountains and central Idaho Panhandle mountains on Tuesday, dry \nand cool conditions will persist through Wednesday. /vmt",
